[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) is marked known: one bug, one fix queued. Its reports say otherwise.
They were merged on subject line alone ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)),
and three different problems share those subjects. The queued parser fix closes one of them.

<Callout kind="warn">
Not one issue. Three problems, three tickets. Do not close #4471 when the parser fix ships.
</Callout>

## What is in #4471

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {issues} />

Every count above is computed from the ticket export at render time, after dropping
reports the export lists twice. Nobody typed a number into this page.

## The three problems

**`parser`: the shared parser reads every date month first.** Every tenant on file writes the
day first, so their dates come out with day and month swapped: 7 April becomes 4 July. It
reaches people through the app, by email and through `booking-v2` forms. This is the bug the
queued fix is for.

**`hint`: the `booking-v3` form tells people to type month first.** Its date field shows
`MM/DD/YYYY`, so people type the month first, and the form reads the date the tenant's way, day first.
The date flips before the parser sees it. Only reports that come in through a `booking-v3` form
belong here. Fixing the parser does not fix the hint, so these keep coming after #4471's fix ships.

**`one-day move`: a date lands one day off, not flipped.** "Tuesday 12 May" shows as "Monday
11 May". A client in Lisbon and a client in Toronto see different days for the same meeting.
An invite arrives before the booking was made. Nothing is swapped, so the parser fix will not
touch it. It looks like a time zone conversion. These are the reports the desk already read as
a one-day move (`desk-notes.json`) and still filed under `parser` by tenant.

## Why the tags do not help

Intake tags a report from the reporter's browser, not from the tenant or the way the report
came in. The table on #4471 shows how far the tags are from the causes:

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

## What changes on the desk

- Keep the three tickets apart. A new report about a wrong date goes to the problem its tenant,
  channel and body point to, not to the ticket with the same subject.
- When the parser fix ships, check the `hint` and `one-day move` tickets before you close anything.
- Subject matching keeps merging these together. Decision 0001 stands until a new record replaces it.

